Here's a JavaScript function that checks if every opening bracket has a corresponding closing bracket:

```javascript
const correctBracketing = (brackets) => {
  let stack = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < brackets.length; i++) {
    if (brackets[i] === '(') {
      stack.push('(');
    } else if (brackets[i] === ')') {
      if (stack.length === 0) {
        return false;
      }
      stack.pop();
    }
  }

  return stack.length === 0;
};

console.log(correctBracketing("(")); // false
console.log(correctBracketing("()")); // true
console.log(correctBracketing("(()())")); // true
console.log(correctBracketing(")(()")); // false
```

This function uses a stack to keep track of opening brackets. Whenever it encounters an opening bracket, it pushes it onto the stack. When it encounters a closing bracket, it checks if the stack is empty. If the stack is empty, it means there is no corresponding opening bracket, so the function returns false. Otherwise, it pops the top element from the stack. After iterating through the entire string, the function checks if the stack is empty. If the stack is empty, it means all opening brackets have corresponding closing brackets, so the function returns true. Otherwise, it returns false.

