```go
func IsSimplePower(x int, n int) bool {
    // Check if n is 0 or x is less than 1
    if n == 0 || x < 1 {
        return false
    }

    // Check if x is a power of n
    for i := 0; i <= 100; i++ { // assuming the power will not exceed 100
        if n**i == x {
            return true
        }
        if n**i > x {
            return false
        }
    }
    return false
}
```

However, the above solution is not efficient for large inputs because it checks all powers up to 100. A more efficient solution would be to use a logarithmic approach:

```go
import math

func IsSimplePower(x int, n int) bool {
    // Check if n is 0 or x is less than 1
    if n == 0 || x < 1 {
        return false
    }

    // Calculate the logarithm of x with base n
    log := math.Log(float64(x)) / math.Log(float64(n))

    // Check if the logarithm is an integer
    return math.Abs(log-math.Floor(log)) < 1e-9
}
```

This solution works because if `x` is a power of `n`, then the logarithm of `x` with base `n` will be an integer. The `math.Abs(log-math.Floor(log)) < 1e-9` check is used to account for floating point precision errors.

